entry.summary:
Most of my projects are small enough that a build pipeline would be more moving parts than the project itself. One of them is a static operations dashboard served by nginx from a single directory, and that directory is also the git checkout. There is no release step and no copy into place, which changes what the word deploy means.
nginx reads the site from the checkout itself:
server_name agentjj.com.br;
root /var/www/central_operacoes;

That path is a working tree with a branch, a remote and 645 commits of history, and nginx serves whatever is on disk at the moment of the request. I edit a file, refresh the page, and the change is live for whoever has the password. The deploy script does not move files into place, because they are already there. It proves that what is on disk behaves, and…
